# Dinchy agent rules

Always-on principles for working in this codebase. These are the rules; the full
usage of a package lives in its godoc — run `go doc <pkg>` — and domain vocabulary
lives in `CONTEXT.md`. Each area below carries a short example and points to the
godoc that documents its mechanics in depth.

## Core principles

* Prioritize correctness and clarity. If a choice trades correctness for speed, choose correctness; if a change introduces ambiguity, prefer the option easier to reason about later.
* Prefer working in existing files and packages unless you are introducing a new logical component. Keep related code close to the feature it serves; avoid new top-level structure unless the existing one cannot hold the change cleanly.
* Avoid drive-by refactors and unrelated cleanup. Keep the change scoped; if cleanup is necessary, do it with clear purpose and a clean end state, not shims or incidental patches.
* Keep comments rare — only for non-obvious intent, a trap, or a constraint not already clear from the code. This governs inline comments, not Go doc comments: every exported symbol and package must still carry a terse, intent-focused Go doc comment (enforced by `revive`).
* Never silently discard errors. If an error is intentionally ignored, make that explicit; if it is not actionable, preserve enough context to explain why.
* Never log and return the same error. Log it if this layer is the end of the line for observability; return it if the caller still needs to decide how to handle it.

## Errors

* Always add context when returning an error, and make that context uniquely identify the code path when multiple returns can fail in the same function. Never return a bare `err` when a richer error is available; if there is genuinely no new context, leave a brief comment saying why the existing error suffices.
* Use `internal/foundation/errors.AppError` for user-visible failures and anything crossing a layer boundary. Prefer the typed constructors (`BadRequest`, `Unauthorized`, `Forbidden`, `Conflict`, `UnprocessableEntity`, `TooManyRequests`, `Internal`) and keep the stable code and localized `i18n` message at the source:
  * `return apperrors.Conflict(i18n.Msg(i18n.CodeAccountAuthInvitationExists))`
* Annotate as the error travels up; do not replace it. Preserve the original code and metadata, and convert an unstructured error into a structured internal one at the first boundary that can add context:
  * `return apperrors.Annotate(err, apperrors.WithTask("purge-sessions"))`
* Attach metadata through the typed `With*` helpers, not ad hoc maps. Any new user-visible error must add or reuse the matching `i18n.Message` and `i18n.Code` in the same change.
* Leaf helpers and parsers that do not cross a layer boundary may return plain errors or sentinel booleans; keep that exception narrow and do not let raw errors leak past a boundary.
* Raw `fmt.Errorf` is only for internal diagnostic causes inside `WithCause`. Use `errors.Join` only to preserve two failures that must both survive (a primary plus a rollback failure); wrap with `%w` only when exposing the underlying error is deliberate. In cause strings use `%q` for user input, IDs, and paths, and `%T` when the concrete type is part of the failure.
* Do not start error text with `failed to` or `error` (that prefix is for logs); write the action or condition instead.
* Prefer returning errors all the way up. Only `main` should call `fatal`.
* Usage — constructors, `Annotate`, metadata helpers, the log-once marker, the i18n workflow: `go doc ./internal/foundation/errors`.

## Logging

* Log each application event once, at the boundary that owns it. Do not log the same event in a callee and its caller.
* Use `Info` for completed milestones and state transitions; use `Warn` for recoverable anomalies and fallbacks that do not fail the operation. If the condition should fail the request or process, return an error instead of warning.
* Message style: sentence case, present tense for starting or stopping actions and past tense for completed outcomes, capitalize acronyms (`HTTP`, `URL`, `ID`), no trailing period. Prefer stable structured fields (`component`, `operation`, `task`, `status`, `duration`, `request_id`, `trace_id`, `span_id`) over free-form text:
  * `logging.Info(ctx, logger, "worker run completed", slog.String("task", task))`
* Error logging is boundary-only and goes solely through `internal/platform/logging.Error` / `logging.HTTPError`; never call `slog` error methods with an error value outside that package. HTTP handlers log only the final 5xx failure at the transport boundary.
* `AppError` carries a log-once marker, and `go run ./cmd/validate logreturn ./...` enforces that no function both logs and returns an error (exempt a deliberate dual-failure path with `//dinchy:allow-logreturn <reason>`).
* Usage — `Info`/`Warn` guidance, message style, boundary-only error logging: `go doc ./internal/platform/logging`.

## Conventions

* Keep feature-local helpers, handlers, services, and types together under the same feature package when that reduces indirection.
* Feature files use the approved `*_*.go` classes (`*_service.go`, `*_helpers.go`, `*_middleware.go`, `*_type.go`, `handler.go`, `*_background_job.go`, `events.json`, `events_generated.go`) plus `*_mockdata_test.go` for mock data. A file fitting none of these is a design decision, not a naming accident; a new class must be approved and recorded here first.
* Define API input validation and normalization at the transport boundary; implement `Resolve` to canonicalize input via `internal/foundation/transform`, and keep handlers and services operating on validated, transformed values only.
* All environment variables must come from `internal/config`; do not define env var names ad hoc.
* Tests cover caller-visible behavior (error codes, metadata, responses), not implementation trivia. Name test files after the parent feature or source file they validate.
* Usage — feature file classes, the `Resolve` pattern, config, test naming: `go doc ./internal/features`.

### Comments

* A comment describes the current state of the code, never its history. Do not narrate what the code used to do, what approach was replaced, or why it changed — the commit message and the diff carry that. When you change code, rewrite or delete the comment that no longer holds.
* Never stack a comment on a comment. One comment per thing being explained; if an existing one is stale or wrong, replace it rather than annotate it.
* A comment that exists to justify an exception, a workaround, or a surprising special case is a signal, not a solution — analyse the root cause and fix it there. It stays only when the constraint is genuinely external (a third-party contract, a protocol quirk, a platform bug), and then the comment names that external constraint, not the local symptom.

### Godoc

* Every package and exported symbol carries a terse, intent-focused doc comment beginning with its name (`// Package errors ...`, `// Annotate preserves ...`), enforced by `revive`. This is the doc-comment side of "keep comments rare": inline comments stay rare, doc comments are always required.
* One package comment per package. Put it on the package's primary source file; use a dedicated `doc.go` (holding only the package comment and any `//go:generate` directive) when there is no natural home file, or when the package warrants a substantial multi-paragraph usage narrative — as in `internal/foundation/errors`, `internal/platform/logging`, and `internal/features`.
* Document utility behavior with testable `Example` functions in `example_test.go` (black-box `package <name>_test`), named so godoc attaches them (`Example`, `ExampleApply`, `ExampleAppError_Status`). End a deterministic example with a trailing `// Output:` block; omit it for non-deterministic output. Reference implementations live in `internal/foundation/{transform,id,errors,security}/example_test.go`.

## Naming and scope

* Use full words for identifiers and variables; avoid abbreviations unless universally understood and materially clearer.
* Name files after the feature or concern they implement, not their internal mechanics.
* Keep new rules high-signal: add one only when it captures a repeated, non-obvious trap. If a naming pattern or workflow changes, get it approved and record it here before using it.

## Reference

* Domain vocabulary and module ownership map — `CONTEXT.md`
* Error handling usage — `go doc ./internal/foundation/errors`
* Logging usage — `go doc ./internal/platform/logging`
* Feature conventions — `go doc ./internal/features`
* Godoc reference implementations — `internal/foundation/{transform,id,errors,security}/example_test.go`
